                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Summary: A game that does not have any stand out features, and those that do are not positive. Note: I did not finish this game, it is possible (but unlikely) that it gets amazing at the end and redeems itself of all criticisms below.\r\nCombat: The combat did not really iterate in any meaningful way over its spiritual inspiration (Chrono Trigger). The game difficulty is also very uneven, I was skating through the game until I bumped into very challenging bosses twice. In one instance, the game does not allow you to do anything else except fight the boss, so I thought I was soft locked. In the other instance, online discourse suggests leveling up about 6 levels from where I was. The game's systems do try to innovate on the genre, but execution is poor. One example is the momentum system in combat: the timing for the button is not consistent so it is easy to miss it, especially if you queue party member actions. Another example is the item system of loot that drops from battles. Some items are freely sellable for currency, some should be retained as ingredients for food and spritnite. Without knowing which is which, it takes a while to realize that green items should be saved. The weapon upgrade system is also explained early, but there is no point interacting with it for at least half of the game since a weapon upgrade/sidegrade is available at almost each new town\r\nMusic: The piano-only music is clearly a conscious decision; however, I think completely misses the mark and sounds very underdeveloped, as if the studio did not hire a music producer or full suite of instruments.\r\nLevel design: This is also largely uninspired; in the same way that many medieval fantasy games can be criticized as over use of grays and browns, the snow setting of this game means that it is mostly whites. The game also makes the brave choice of having white sparkles for items, on the aforementioned white palette ground, making these difficult to find. If you stand very close to them, the controller will vibrate, but this could have been completely avoided with a different design choice. There was one area that stood out as different, potentially implying an advanced technology under a cave/mountain, with levitating platforms, but there was no explanation about this area.\r\nLore: The game's story teases interest, although it falls into very tired tropes (mysterious shadowy dark pursuer) and is uneven in narrative beats (I felt like I played very long sessions without getting any character interaction or story progression. The story doesn't seem inherently bad, but it is not enough to hold a player through lacking gameplay.\r\nConclusion: This game is a disappointment that I don't have the interest in playing through fully, let alone playing through multiple times. The aspects of the game familiar to turn based jrpg gamers do not distinguish themselves positively from peers of the past (Chrono Trigger, Lost Odyssey) or more recent entires to the genre (Octopath Traveler, Claire Obscur), and its experimental ideas are executed poorly. With all its flaws, I rate this a 2/10.",
